\frametitle{Structural Causal Models}
A structural causal model (SCM) consists of structural assignments
$$
X_i := f_i(\pa_i,\epsilon_i)\qquad (i=1,\ldots,m),
$$
where $\pa_i\subset \{X_1,\ldots,X_m\}\setminus \{X_i\}$ and all $\epsilon_i$ are jointly independent.\\[.2cm]
The underlying graph $G$ is defined in the obvious way.
\begin{alertblock}{If $G$ is a DAG, simulating from an SCM is easy}
\begin{lstlisting}
set.seed(1); n <- 100
X3 <- runif(n)-0.5
X1 <- X3*X3 + rnorm(n)
X2 <- sin(X1) + rnorm(n)^2
X4 <- X2 + exp(X3 + rnorm(n))
X  <- cbind(X1,X2,X3,X4)
\end{lstlisting}
\end{alertblock}
